course: dictionary
lesson: 1390
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and «capture» is one of them. You cannot pick the right meaning from the word alone. Look at the words around it, because they are its context and they give you the clue.
explain: «The Ottoman sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople in 1453.» Here capture means to take control of a place by force, as an army does in a battle. The clue is that a city was taken over by soldiers.
explain: «In a video call the camera captures your face.» Here capture means to record something, like a photo or film. Nothing is caught or held prisoner. The camera just keeps the picture.
explain: In chess, to capture means to take an opponent's piece off the board. So when you meet «capture», ask yourself: what is being taken, and is it a place, a picture, or a piece in a game?
fact: Capture can be a verb (to capture) or a noun (a capture).
fact: «Capture» can mean to catch a person or animal so it cannot escape.
fact: «Capture» can mean to take control of a place by force, as armies did with castles and towns.
fact: «Capture» can mean to record something in a photo, film or words, as in «capture a moment».
fact: «Capture» can also mean to hold someone's attention or interest, as in «capture someone's imagination».
exercise: «The Ottoman sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople in 1453.» What does captured mean here? | Caught a fish in the harbour | *Took control of the city by force | Made a sketch of its walls | Took a photo of the city
exercise: «In a video call the camera captures your face.» What does capture mean here? | To catch and keep a person | To take control of a place by force | To take a piece in a game | *To record something in a picture
exercise: In chess, what does it mean to capture a piece? | To move it one square | *To take it off the board | To give it to your friend | To hide it under the table
test: «Malacca grew into one of Asia's greatest ports, and the Portuguese captured it in 1511.» Which meaning fits best? | *Took control of the place by force | Caught a fish in the sea | Moved it to a new port | Recorded a picture of the port
test: The story says the hero was captured, then escaped. What does captured mean? | Recorded on film | *Caught and held so it could not get away | Took a piece in a game | Liked very much
exam: Tom says «capture» always means taking a photo. What is the best answer? | Yes, it only means taking a photo | Yes, it only ever means playing games | No, it means eating | *No, it can also mean catching a person or taking control of a place
